WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.017
Random value length should be selected intentionally.

2
00:00:03.017 --> 00:00:11.170
Longer values can increase guessing resistance and reduce collision risk,
but they may affect storage, URLs, human handling, and support workflows.

3
00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:20.478
Shorter values may be easier for users to type, but they usually need
additional controls such as expiration, throttling, attempt limits,
monitoring, and narrow purpose.

4
00:00:20.478 --> 00:00:24.908
Copying an example length without understanding the use case is a common
mistake.

5
00:00:24.908 --> 00:00:27.732
Encoding changes how random bytes are represented.

6
00:00:27.732 --> 00:00:33.574
Hex, base64-style encodings, URL-safe encodings, and human-friendly
character sets all have tradeoffs.

7
00:00:33.574 --> 00:00:35.115
Some encodings are compact.

8
00:00:35.115 --> 00:00:36.976
Some are easier to paste into URLs.

9
00:00:36.976 --> 00:00:38.838
Some avoid ambiguous characters.

10
00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:41.470
Encoding is not the source of security by itself.

11
00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:48.146
The underlying unpredictability and the number of possible values matter
more than whether the output looks complicated.

12
00:00:48.146 --> 00:00:55.336
Modulo bias at a high level occurs when a method maps random values into a
range in a way that makes some outputs more likely than others.

13
00:00:55.336 --> 00:01:02.911
This can happen when developers take a random number and reduce it with a
simple remainder operation without considering the distribution.

14
00:01:02.911 --> 00:01:11.064
The safer approach is to use library functions designed to choose
uniformly from a range or character set, especially for security-sensitive
values.

15
00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:20.501
Predictable prefixes, truncation mistakes, and fixed patterns can reduce
effective strength or leak information about purpose, environment, tenant,
or generation time.

16
00:01:20.501 --> 00:01:25.251
Prefixes may be useful for operations, but they should not be counted as
secret entropy.

17
00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:30.836
If a token includes a readable label, the random part still needs enough
unpredictability for the risk.

18
00:01:30.836 --> 00:01:37.320
Design the format deliberately, then test lifecycle behavior rather than
trying to test randomness quality directly.
